<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bignor Roman Villa</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Bignor Roman Villa is the site of a large Roman courtyard villa near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chichester">Chichester</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-sussex">West Sussex</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, discovered in 1811. Developed from a modest farmhouse into one of the largest villas in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> by the fourth century, it is known for its fine mosaics, including scenes of Venus and gladiatorial cupids and a head of Medusa, which are still displayed in their original positions.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bignor-roman-villa.jpg" alt="Bignor Roman Villa" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Trevor Harris · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bignor_Roman_Villa_-_geograph.org.uk_-_3599416.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the countryside the elite built villas, from modest farmhouses to the great palace at Fishbourne of about 75, and in the 4th century houses such as Chedworth and <b>Bignor</b> reached their greatest size and finest mosaics.</blockquote><a href="/empire/roman-britain">Roman Britain · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
